{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-inference.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Type inference",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the most general type of a term whose binders have no types, by solving the equations its applications impose.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Without a type on its binder, $\\lambda x.\\, x$ has the type $A \\to A$, and $B \\to B$, and $(A \\to B) \\to A \\to B$: no single answer, until we ask for the best one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Find a type for $\\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$ by hand. What were you forced to decide, and what could you leave open?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Unknowns and equations",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Treat the unknown types like unknowns in algebra. Say $f : \\tau_1$ and $x : \\tau_2$, and call the type of $f\\ x$ $\\tau_3$. Only the application forces anything: $f\\ x$ types when $f$ is a function from $x$'s type to the result's, $\\tau_1 = \\tau_2 \\to \\tau_3$. Put that in, and the term has type $(\\tau_2 \\to \\tau_3) \\to \\tau_2 \\to \\tau_3$. Nothing constrains $\\tau_2$ and $\\tau_3$, so they stay variables, $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λf. λx. f x",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Equations can meet. In $\\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ (f\\ x)$, $f$ is applied twice, and each application gives an equation for $f$'s type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Predict the type: what does applying $f$ to its own result force?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λf. λx. f (f x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "Two arrows are equal only when their arguments are equal and their results are, so $\\tau_2 \\to \\tau_3 = \\tau_3 \\to \\tau_4$ splits into $\\tau_2 = \\tau_3$ and $\\tau_3 = \\tau_4$: $f$ must return the type it takes. The answer, $(\\alpha \\to \\alpha) \\to \\alpha \\to \\alpha$, is the type of the Church numeral $2$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Inference\n> 1. Give each binder without a type, and each application's result, a **type variable** $\\tau_1, \\tau_2, \\ldots$.\n> 2. Each application $M\\ N$ gives an equation: $M$'s type $= N$'s type $\\to$ the result's.\n> 3. Solve the equations one at a time (**unification**): an equation $\\tau = T$ puts $T$ for $\\tau$ everywhere; two arrows are equal when their arguments and their results are.\n> 4. The variables left are named $\\alpha, \\beta, \\ldots$: any types put for them give a type of the term.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$S$, three binders and three applications, goes the same way:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: S",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The most general type",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The variables left over are a promise: any types put for them give a type of the term. $K$ comes out as $\\alpha \\to \\beta \\to \\alpha$,",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: K",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "so $A \\to B \\to A$ and $(A \\to A) \\to B \\to A \\to A$ are types of $K$ too: instances of it. Unification never commits to more than the equations force, so the type it finds is the **principal type**: every type of the term is an instance of it (Hindley, 1969). One answer covers them all.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The occurs check",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 2 showed that $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ has no type by trying guesses. Inference finds it in one equation: $x : \\tau_1$ applied to itself gives $\\tau_1 = \\tau_1 \\to \\tau_2$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λx. x x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake] Just substitute\n> Putting $\\tau_1 \\to \\tau_2$ for $\\tau_1$ never ends: $\\tau_1 = (\\tau_1 \\to \\tau_2) \\to \\tau_2 = ((\\tau_1 \\to \\tau_2) \\to \\tau_2) \\to \\tau_2 = \\cdots$. A variable cannot be solved by a type that contains it, so unification checks for that (the **occurs check**) and gives up. Allowing the infinite type instead would give $\\Omega$ a type.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Some types given",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Types given and types found mix: annotated binders keep their types, and the rest is inferred around them. A free variable gets a type variable too, and the answer says what it had to be.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λf:A→B. λx. f x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: f x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every inferred type is checked: the term, annotated with it, goes through the type checker of lesson 1, and the tree shown is that check.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean infers too, by the same kind of unification: give it some types and it finds the rest.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "#check fun (f : Nat → Bool) x => f x\n#check fun (x : Nat) (_ : String) => x",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem typing_by_inference : ∃ T, HasType [] (.lam \"f\" (A ⇒ B) (.lam \"x\" A (.app (.var \"f\") (.var \"x\")))) T := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Show that $\\lambda f{:}A \\to B.\\, \\lambda x{:}A.\\, f\\ x$ has a type, without writing it: let Lean infer it from the derivation.",
      "leanSolution": "  exact ⟨_, .abs (.abs (.app (.var rfl) (.var rfl)))⟩",
      "hints": [
        "`exact ⟨_, derivation⟩`: the underscore is solved by unification with the derivation's conclusion.",
        "The derivation is →I, →I, then →E over two Vars."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Answer with letters for the type variables, like `a -> b -> a`; any names will do, as long as the shape matches.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λx. λy. y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, y$.",
      "hints": [
        "Two arguments of unrelated types; it returns the second."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λf. λg. λx. g (f x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of composition, $\\lambda f.\\, \\lambda g.\\, \\lambda x.\\, g\\ (f\\ x)$.",
      "hints": [
        "$x : \\alpha$, $f : \\alpha \\to \\beta$, $g : \\beta \\to \\gamma$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λx. λf. f x",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda f.\\, f\\ x$.",
      "hints": [
        "$f$ is applied to $x$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "infer: λx. λy. x (y x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Infer the type of $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x\\ (y\\ x)$, by writing its two equations and solving them.",
      "hints": [
        "Say $x : \\tau_1$, $y : \\tau_2$. Then $y\\ x$ gives $\\tau_2 = \\tau_1 \\to \\tau_3$, and $x\\ (y\\ x)$ gives $\\tau_1 = \\tau_3 \\to \\tau_4$.",
        "Put the second into the first: $y : (\\tau_3 \\to \\tau_4) \\to \\tau_3$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Inference gives each unknown type a variable, turns each application into an equation, and solves the equations by unification. The occurs check rejects a type that would contain itself. The answer is the principal type: every other type of the term is an instance of it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ has no simple type, so neither do $\\Omega$ and $Y$, the terms that loop. Are they the only terms types rule out, and can a typed term ever run for ever?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
